<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Selma to Montgomery marches</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Selma to Montgomery march</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Selma to Montgomery marches were three protest marches for African American voting rights, held in 1965 along the 54-mile (87 km) highway from Selma, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, to Montgomery, the state capital. On 7 March, &quot;Bloody Sunday&quot;, state troopers and a county posse attacked an estimated 525 to 600 marchers with batons and tear gas at the Edmund Pettus Bridge. The third march, under federal protection, reached Montgomery on 24 March; the next day 25,000 people demonstrated at the Alabama State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>. The marches contributed to the passage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches.jpg" alt="Selma to Montgomery marches" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abernathy Family · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abernathy_Children_on_front_line_leading_the_SELMA_TO_MONTGOMERY_MARCH_for_the_RIGHT_TO_VOTE.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
